window.__labLoaded({"id":"9.1","title":"Study Design: How the Data Were Collected Decides What You Can Claim","goal":"Simulate 200 people with a hidden fitness level and see that a coin-flip assignment balances it between two groups while self-selection does not, which biases the estimated effect.","question":"A city health office wants to know whether its new fitness programme really improves people's health scores. It could let residents sign up if they like, or it could flip a coin to decide who gets in. To see which design can be trusted, we invent 200 people, give the programme a true effect of +5 points that we know, and check which design gets close to 5.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Create people with a hidden trait","text":"We invent 200 people. Each has a fitness score that we, as researchers, would not normally see. The outcome (say, a health score) depends on fitness and on a program that truly adds 5 points. A seed makes the simulation repeatable.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nn = 200\ntrue_effect = 5\n\nfitness = rng.normal(50, 10, n)\nnoise = rng.normal(0, 5, n)\nbase_outcome = 40 + 0.5 * fitness + noise\n\nprint(f'people = {n}, mean fitness = {fitness.mean():.1f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for numbers and random draws.","Create a random number generator with a fixed seed (42), so every run gives the same 'random' people and the lab is repeatable.","The number of people in our invented study.","The programme's true effect, which we set ourselves at +5 points. In real life nobody knows this.","Give each person a hidden fitness level, drawn from a normal curve with mean 50 and SD 10.","Draw random noise (SD 5) for everything else that affects health, such as luck and genes.","Health score without the programme: it depends on fitness (0.5 points per fitness point) plus noise.","Print the number of people and their mean fitness as a check that the simulation worked."],"meaning":"We made 200 people with a mean fitness of 49.7. Fitness is hidden in real studies, but here we know it. It raises the health score, so any group that is fitter at the start will also look healthier, whether or not the programme works.","out":"people = 200, mean fitness = 49.7","figs":[]},{"title":"Random assignment: a coin decides","text":"Each person is put in the program or the comparison group by a coin flip, so fitness plays no part. With enough people, the two groups come out alike on fitness, and on every other trait we did not even measure.","code":"random_group = rng.random(n) < 0.5\n\nprint('RANDOM ASSIGNMENT')\nprint(f'  program    : n = {random_group.sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[random_group].mean():.1f}')\nprint(f'  comparison : n = {(~random_group).sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[~random_group].mean():.1f}')","explain":["Flip a fair coin for every person: draw a random number between 0 and 1, and put the person in the programme if it is below 0.5.","Print a heading so the output is labelled.","Show the programme group's size and its mean fitness. ~ is the 'not' operator and means the comparison group on the next line.","Show the comparison group's size and mean fitness. If the coin worked, the two means should be close."],"meaning":"The coin put 95 people in the programme and 105 in the comparison group. Their mean fitness is 49.0 and 50.3, only 1.3 apart, so the groups are alike. Randomisation also balances traits we did not measure, which is its great strength.","out":"RANDOM ASSIGNMENT\n  program    : n = 95, mean fitness = 49.0\n  comparison : n = 105, mean fitness = 50.3","figs":[]},{"title":"Self-selection: people choose","text":"Now fitter people are more likely to sign up (a logistic curve turns fitness into a chance of choosing the program). The groups now differ on fitness before the program has done anything.","code":"chance = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(fitness - 50) / 5))\nchose = rng.random(n) < chance\n\nprint('SELF-SELECTION')\nprint(f'  program    : n = {chose.sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[chose].mean():.1f}')\nprint(f'  comparison : n = {(~chose).sum()}, mean fitness = {fitness[~chose].mean():.1f}')","explain":["Make the chance of signing up rise with fitness. The logistic curve turns fitness into a probability between 0 and 1.","Let each person choose: they join if a random number falls below their own chance. Fitter people join more often.","Print a heading so the output is labelled.","Show the self-selected programme group's size and mean fitness.","Show the comparison group's size and mean fitness. Now the groups should differ before the programme has done anything."],"meaning":"When people choose, 98 joined and 102 did not, but the programme group is much fitter: 54.6 against 45.0, a gap of 9.6 points. The groups were different before the programme started, which is confounding.","out":"SELF-SELECTION\n  program    : n = 98, mean fitness = 54.6\n  comparison : n = 102, mean fitness = 45.0","figs":[]},{"title":"Compare the estimated effects","text":"The program adds exactly 5 points to whoever gets it. Randomised groups should estimate about 5. In the self-selected groups, the program group was fitter to begin with, so the simple difference over-claims.","code":"for label, group in [('random', random_group), ('self-selected', chose)]:\n    outcome = base_outcome + true_effect * group\n    diff = outcome[group].mean() - outcome[~group].mean()\n    print(f'{label:14s} estimated effect = {diff:5.1f}   (true effect = {true_effect})')","explain":["Loop over both designs, each as a name and a True/False list saying who was in the programme.","Each person's outcome is their base score plus 5 points if they were in the programme (True counts as 1).","The estimated effect is the programme group's mean outcome minus the comparison group's. This is what a simple study would report.","Print the estimate beside the true effect of 5, so we can see how close each design gets."],"meaning":"The programme truly adds 5 points. Random assignment estimates 4.5, close to the truth (the gap from 5 is just chance). Self-selection estimates 10.7, more than double, because the fitter people who joined would have scored higher anyway. The design, not the maths, caused the bias.","out":"random         estimated effect =   4.5   (true effect = 5)\nself-selected  estimated effect =  10.7   (true effect = 5)","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"set.seed(42)\nn <- 200\nfitness <- rnorm(n, 50, 10)\nbase <- 40 + 0.5 * fitness + rnorm(n, 0, 5)\nrandom <- runif(n) < 0.5\nchose <- runif(n) < plogis((fitness - 50) / 5)\ntapply(fitness, random, mean)\ntapply(fitness, chose, mean)\nmean((base + 5 * random)[random]) - mean((base + 5 * random)[!random])\nmean((base + 5 * chose)[chose]) - mean((base + 5 * chose)[!chose])","expect":"Random groups have almost the same mean fitness (a gap of a point or two at most) and the estimated effect is close to 5. The self-selected program group has clearly higher mean fitness (about 8 to 10 points) and the estimated effect is far above 5, around 10. Exact numbers differ from Python.","note":"R uses a different random number generator than Python, so the simulated numbers are close in spirit but not identical. The pattern (balanced when random, unbalanced when chosen) is the same.","explain":["Fix the random number generator so every run gives the same numbers.","The number of people in our invented study.","Give each person a hidden fitness level from a normal curve with mean 50 and SD 10.","Health score without the programme: 40 plus 0.5 x fitness plus random noise with SD 5.","Coin flip for each person: runif gives a random number from 0 to 1, and TRUE (in the programme) means it fell below 0.5.","Self-selection: plogis turns fitness into a chance between 0 and 1, and fitter people are more likely to join.","Mean fitness in each group made by the coin. FALSE is the comparison group and TRUE the programme group.","The same group means for the self-selected groups. This time the two means should differ clearly.","Estimated effect with random groups: outcome is base + 5 for those in the programme, then programme mean minus comparison mean.","The same estimate for the self-selected groups, to compare with the true effect of 5."],"read":"The first tapply shows nearly equal mean fitness in the FALSE and TRUE groups (a point or two apart), and the first estimate is near 5. The second tapply shows clearly higher fitness in the TRUE group (about 8 to 10 points), and the second estimate is far above 5 (around 10). Exact numbers differ from Python."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (with Random Number functions), then Analyze > Compare Means > Means","syntax":"SET SEED=42.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP #i = 1 TO 200.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nCOMPUTE fitness = RV.NORMAL(50,10).\nCOMPUTE random = (RV.UNIFORM(0,1) < 0.5).\nCOMPUTE chose = (RV.UNIFORM(0,1) < 1/(1+EXP(-(fitness-50)/5))).\nMEANS TABLES=fitness BY random /CELLS=MEAN COUNT.\nMEANS TABLES=fitness BY chose /CELLS=MEAN COUNT.","note":"SPSS draws different random numbers from Python, so simulated numbers are close but not identical. Compare the mean fitness of the two groups in each MEANS table: nearly equal for random, clearly different for chose. The estimated-effect step is left to Python, R and Stata.","explain":["Fix the random number generator so a rerun gives the same numbers.","Start a block that creates data from nothing, since we are simulating people.","Repeat the steps below 200 times, once per person. #i is a temporary counter.","Each time round, add one new empty row (one person) to the data.","End the repeating block.","Tell SPSS there are no more rows.","End the block. We now have 200 empty rows to fill.","Give each person a hidden fitness level from a normal curve with mean 50 and SD 10.","Coin flip: 1 (in the programme) if a random number from 0 to 1 falls below 0.5, otherwise 0.","Self-selection: the chance of joining rises with fitness, using the logistic formula, and each person joins if a random number falls below it.","Mean fitness and group size for the coin-flip groups. They should be almost equal.","Mean fitness and group size for the self-selected groups. They should differ clearly."],"read":"In the first MEANS table the two groups have nearly equal mean fitness. In the second the group with chose = 1 (joined) has clearly higher mean fitness than chose = 0. Exact numbers differ from Python."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset seed 42\nset obs 200\ngenerate fitness = rnormal(50, 10)\ngenerate base = 40 + 0.5 * fitness + rnormal(0, 5)\ngenerate random = runiform() < 0.5\ngenerate chose = runiform() < invlogit((fitness - 50) / 5)\ntabstat fitness, by(random) statistics(mean n)\ntabstat fitness, by(chose) statistics(mean n)\ngenerate outcome_r = base + 5 * random\ngenerate outcome_c = base + 5 * chose\nttest outcome_r, by(random)\nttest outcome_c, by(chose)","note":"Stata draws different random numbers from Python, so simulated numbers are close but not identical. ttest shows mean(0) - mean(1), so the program effect appears with a negative sign (about -5 when random, a larger negative number when chosen).","explain":["Clear any data in memory.","Fix the random number generator so a rerun gives the same numbers.","Create 200 empty rows, one per person.","Give each person a hidden fitness level from a normal curve with mean 50 and SD 10.","Health score without the programme: 40 plus 0.5 x fitness plus random noise with SD 5.","Coin flip: random is 1 (in the programme) if a random number from 0 to 1 is below 0.5, otherwise 0.","Self-selection: invlogit turns fitness into a chance of joining between 0 and 1, and fitter people join more often.","Mean fitness and number of people in each coin-flip group. They should be nearly equal.","Mean fitness and number of people in each self-selected group. They should differ clearly.","Outcome for the coin-flip design: base score plus the true +5 for those in the programme.","Outcome for the self-selected design, built the same way.","t-test of the outcome between the coin-flip groups. The estimated effect is the difference in means.","The same test for the self-selected groups. The difference should be much larger than 5."],"read":"The first tabstat shows nearly equal mean fitness; the second shows clearly higher fitness for chose = 1. In the ttest tables, 'diff' is mean(0) - mean(1), so the programme effect appears as a negative number: about -5 for random groups and a larger negative number (around -10) for self-selected groups."},"conclusion":"Random assignment gave an estimate of 4.5, close to the true +5, because a coin flip balanced fitness between the groups (49.0 against 50.3). Letting people choose gave 10.7, more than double, because fitter people joined and would have scored higher anyway. Caution: this is a simulation with one hidden trait, and random assignment works best with many people (here 200), not guaranteed with few.","practice":{"scenario":"A school offers a free tutoring programme that truly adds 3 points to a test score. Students may join if they wish, and the more motivated join more often. Simulate 400 students and see how well a coin flip and self-selection recover the true +3.","data":"No data to paste. Simulate: rng = np.random.default_rng(7); n = 400; motivation ~ Normal(50, 10); base score = 60 + 0.6 x motivation + Normal(0, 6); chance of joining = 1 / (1 + exp(-(motivation - 50) / 4)).","tasks":["Simulate the students and assign them by a coin flip (probability 0.5).","Let students choose, using the chance of joining above.","For each design, find the gap in mean motivation and the estimated programme effect (true effect +3).","Which design can you trust, and why?"],"hint":"Copy the lab's structure: draw the trait, build the base outcome, create two True/False group lists, then compare group means for the trait and for outcome = base + 3 x group.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(7)\nn = 400\nmotivation = rng.normal(50, 10, n)\nbase = 60 + 0.6 * motivation + rng.normal(0, 6, n)\ncoin = rng.random(n) < 0.5\nchose = rng.random(n) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(motivation - 50) / 4))\nfor label, g in [('coin flip', coin), ('self-chosen', chose)]:\n    y = base + 3 * g\n    gap = motivation[g].mean() - motivation[~g].mean()\n    print(f'{label:12s} motivation gap = {gap:5.1f}   estimated effect = {y[g].mean() - y[~g].mean():5.1f}')","answer":"With the coin flip the groups differ by only -0.6 in motivation and the estimated effect is 2.8, close to the true +3. With self-selection the programme group is 11.9 points more motivated and the estimate is 10.2, more than three times the truth. Only the randomised design can be trusted to estimate the effect, because it balances motivation (and every unmeasured trait) between the groups.","out":"coin flip    motivation gap =  -0.6   estimated effect =   2.8\nself-chosen  motivation gap =  11.9   estimated effect =  10.2","figs":[]}}});
